<apex:page showHeader="false" sidebar="false" standardStylesheets="false"
           applyHtmlTag="false" applyBodyTag="false" docType="html-5.0"
           title="Messaging | Curb Cut" cache="true" expires="300">
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8"/>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
  <meta name="description" content="How the Curb Cut text message programme works: who it is for, how you opt in, exactly what you will receive, and how to stop."/>
  <title>Messaging | Curb Cut</title>
</head>
<body>
<c:CurbCutShell pageTitle="Messaging" current="messaging"
  eyebrow="The text message programme"
  heading="You text first. That is the only way in."
  lede="Nobody is added to a list here. There is no list.">

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-you-send-the-first">
    <div class="plain">
      <span class="tag">In one sentence</span>
      <p>
        Curb Cut is a workplace accessibility assistant. You text it to find out
        what adjustments you could ask for at work, and it can draft a request
        and send it to your employer &mdash; but only if you approve it first,
        in the conversation. It never messages anyone who has not messaged it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-you-send-the-first-2">
    <p class="sec-label">The programme</p>
    <table class="facts">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Programme name</th><td>Curb Cut</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Operated by</th><td>Parth Sevak, Welland, Ontario, Canada</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Number</th><td><span class="kw">+1 276 495 9311</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Who it is for</th>
            <td>Employees, contractors and applicants who want to know what
                workplace adjustments exist and how to ask for one.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Message type</th>
            <td>Conversational replies only. Every message you receive answers a
                message you sent. There is no marketing, no promotion, and no
                recurring or scheduled messaging of any kind.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Message frequency</th>
            <td>Varies, and is entirely determined by you. If you stop texting,
                the messages stop.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost</th>
            <td><b>Message and data rates may apply.</b> Curb Cut charges you
                nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Policies</th>
            <td><a href="/terms">Terms and Conditions</a> &nbsp;
                <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-you-send-the-first-3">
    <p class="sec-label">Opt-in, path one of one</p>
    <h2 id="sec-you-send-the-first-3">id="sec-you-send-the-first-2">id="sec-you-send-the-first">You send the first message, from this notice</h2>
    <p>
      There is exactly one way to start receiving messages from Curb Cut: you
      text the number yourself. The notice below is what is printed and put up
      in workplaces, break rooms and union offices. It is reproduced here
      word for word, including the consent line.
    </p>

    <div class="poster">
      <p class="poster-kicker">Is something at work harder than it needs to be?</p>
      <p class="poster-do">Text CURB CUT to</p>
      <p class="poster-num">+1 276 495 9311</p>
      <p class="poster-why">
        Find out what you could ask for. You do not need an account, a work
        login, or a reason. You never have to say what condition you have.
      </p>
      <p class="poster-legal">
        By texting this number you agree to receive text messages from Curb Cut
        in reply. Message frequency varies. Message and data rates may apply.
        Reply <b>HELP</b> for help, <b>STOP</b> to stop.
        Terms: <a href="/terms">curbcut/terms</a> &middot;
        Privacy: <a href="/privacy">curbcut/privacy</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      That is the whole opt-in. There is no web form, no imported list, no
      purchased data, no third-party lead source, and no box that anyone can
      tick on your behalf. If you have not texted the number, the number will
      never text you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-the-exact-messages-as">
    <p class="sec-label">What actually arrives</p>
    <h2 id="sec-the-exact-messages-as">The exact messages, as sent</h2>
    <p>
      These are quoted from the running code, not paraphrased. The first is sent
      once, immediately after your very first message, as a second message so
      that the answer you asked for arrives before the small print.
    </p>

    <p class="sms-label">Sent once, on your first message</p>
    <div class="sms">Curb Cut: you texted first, so you will get replies from this number. Message frequency varies and replies only follow your own messages. Message and data rates may apply. Reply HELP for help, STOP to stop. Terms https://orgfarm-7a04c62cb9.my.salesforce-sites.com/curbcut/terms Privacy https://orgfarm-7a04c62cb9.my.salesforce-sites.com/curbcut/privacy</div>

    <p class="sms-label">If you reply HELP or INFO</p>
    <div class="sms">Curb Cut helps you find out what could make work easier at work, and ask for it, without ever saying what condition you have. Message and data rates may apply. Reply STOP to stop. Help: parth.sevak2@gmail.com Terms https://orgfarm-7a04c62cb9.my.salesforce-sites.com/curbcut/terms</div>

    <p class="sms-label">If you reply STOP, STOPALL, UNSUBSCRIBE, CANCEL, END or QUIT</p>
    <div class="sms">Curb Cut: you will not get any more messages from this number. Reply START if you ever want to come back. Nothing about you is kept.</div>

    <p class="sms-label">If you later reply START, YES or UNSTOP</p>
    <div class="sms">Curb Cut: you are back. Tell me what is hard at work right now. Message and data rates may apply. Reply HELP for help, STOP to stop.</div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-stop-works-immediately-and">
    <p class="sec-label">Stopping</p>
    <h2 id="sec-stop-works-immediately-and">STOP works, immediately, and you are not asked why</h2>
    <p>
      Reply <span class="kw">STOP</span> at any time. You get one confirmation
      and then nothing further, ever, unless you text again yourself. You will
      not be asked to confirm twice, given a reason to reconsider, or asked what
      went wrong. The system has nowhere to record a reason, so nobody can ask
      you for one later.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Four other words do something different, and none of them stop
        the messages.</strong>
        <span class="kw">HUMAN</span> hands you to a real person, on this same
        channel, at any point.
        <span class="kw">OFF</span> turns off a standing preference you saved,
        and <span class="kw">WHO</span> lists everyone who has been shown it
        &mdash; both take the six-character code you were given when you saved
        it, like <span class="kw">OFF 4KQ7MT</span>, because we hold no record of
        who you are and cannot look you up.
        Inside a conversation, <span class="kw">DELETE</span> discards a drafted
        request before it is sent. The assistant is specifically instructed never
        to tell you to reply STOP for any of these, because STOP belongs to you
        and to your carrier, not to us.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-it-is-hashed-on">
    <p class="sec-label">What we do with your number</p>
    <h2 id="sec-it-is-hashed-on">It is hashed on arrival and never stored</h2>
    <p>
      Your phone number is turned into a one-way salted hash the moment a
      message arrives, and it is that hash, never the number, that is written
      down. It exists so your conversation stays continuous across messages.
      Numbers are never sold, never shared for anyone else's purposes, never
      used for marketing, and never handed to a data broker or an advertiser.
      The full detail is in the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sec-other-ways-in-if">
    <h2 id="sec-other-ways-in-if">Other ways in, if texting is not the one that works for you</h2>
    <p>
      The same assistant answers on <a href="/ask">this website</a> with no
      phone involved at all, by email, and by voice on the same number for
      anyone for whom typing is the hard part. None of them require an account.
    </p>
  </section>

</c:CurbCutShell>
</body>
</html>
</apex:page>
